  "step_3": "The decision reason requires two elements: (1) treating outcome differences as negligible, and (2) preferring the lottery with the higher probability of a favorable outcome. The individual does treat the outcome difference as negligible, which aligns with the first part. However, while the second part of the reason (choosing based on higher probability) is consistent with choosing B, the individual does not mention or reference probabilities at all in their justification. Their reasoning is based on emotional acceptance of the smaller outcome, not on a comparison of chances. Therefore, although the individual might have chosen B (implied), the justification does not reflect the probabilistic comparison required by the reason. The absence of any reference to likelihoods or chances makes it uncertain whether the probability-based rule was used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
